{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:inflation:audit","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:810a6a032f80e3987395fda444b88cdb94026dcef1f54fcc9ad4b5763d4f78c8","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.39},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"RESOLVING SERIES. Conjunct A: \"GDP-weighted OECD-38 core CPI/PCE\". No such series is published. OECD publishes an OECD-total CPI and CPI-excluding-food-and-energy weighted by PRIVATE CONSUMPTION PPP, not GDP. The \"/PCE\" latitude alone is worth +0.1-0.3pp on the US leg (US core CPI has run ~30-40bp above core PCE on shelter weight and formula/scope) and the US is ~40% of OECD-38 PPP GDP. Euro-area \"core\" (HICP ex energy, food, alcohol, tobacco) uses a different exclusion set from US core AND excludes owner-occupied housing entirely; if OOH enters HICP during the window (long-standing Eurostat workstream) euro core steps up +0.1-0.3pp with no change in the world. EM-OECD weight is the biggest single artefact: Turkey, Mexico, Chile, Colombia, Costa Rica are inside OECD-38 and carry structurally higher core inflation; at 2025 PPP GDP weights Turkey alone (~2.3%) at 12-18% core adds ~0.25-0.35pp to the aggregate, and the five together put a floor of roughly +0.30pp under any OECD-38 GDP-weighted core print. Direction: strongly YES. Revisions: CPI is not revised (seasonal factors aside); PCE is revised annually and in benchmark years, by up to 0.2-0.3pp on an annual rate — so a PCE-based resolution is vintage-dependent.\nLEVEL vs SKEW (the effort's core requirement, carried with weights). 2027-2030 impulse: tariff/fragmentation goods pass-through; shelter (33% of US core CPI, ~15% of euro core) running 2.5-3.5% as construction costs compete with grid/data-centre trades; medical+social care services (~7-9% of US core) at 3-5% on care wage scarcity. CRITICAL ARTEFACT: the single most AI-specific inflationary channel — data-centre electricity pass-through — is ENERGY and therefore EXCLUDED from core on both the US and euro definitions. +635 TWh (AI-Q3 P50 ~1,000 vs 365 in 2024) implies ~130-160 GW firm capacity plus transmission, ~$1.3-2.0T over 12 years, i.e. ~20% on top of current OECD+China grid/generation capex — large, zonally violent (PJM, Virginia, Ireland, Osaka), but diluted to perhaps +0.1-0.3pp on OECD-38 HEADLINE and only +0.1-0.2pp on core via second-round business costs. A naive reader double-counts this into core; I do not (-4pp vs naive). 2031-2036 disinflation: the mirror-image artefact runs the other way. ISIC J, K, M are PRODUCER-heavy; AI's output-price deflation lands overwhelmingly in intermediate/B2B services that barely enter a CONSUMER basket. Consumer-facing AI-deflatables are telecom/internet (~1.5-2%), audio-video/media (~1%), education (~2.5%), household legal/accounting (<1%), plus imputed financial services (2-7% of PCE, measured by a method AI will not touch cleanly). Even a total collapse of J/K/M prices moves core CPI by only -0.2 to -0.4pp. So \"AI disinflation\" is structurally under-weighted in the index that resolves this (+6pp on YES vs the narrative reading).\nARITHMETIC OF THE 10-YEAR AVERAGE: the window is FRONT-LOADED. 2027-30 at ~2.8% and 2031-36 at ~2.35% gives a 10-year average of ~2.53% — it clears. The hurdle is met largely by the first four years plus the EM-OECD floor, not by a 2030s stagflation. Conjunct A = 62%.\nConjunct B (China headline CPI < 1.50%/yr, 2027-36). SERIES: NBS monthly CPI, basket weights rebased on a 5-year cycle (2026 is a rebase year — a services-weight increase would nudge prints UP), housing captured as RENT with a crude imputation so the property adjustment barely registers, pork cycle dominating the volatility. Starting point ~0.0-0.3% (2024 +0.2%, 2025 ~0%, repeated negative prints); PPI negative for most of 2022-26. Drivers per the brief, treated semi-independently: property-balance-sheet adjustment, household income share of GDP ~44%, deliberate supply-side industrial policy manufacturing persistent goods overcapacity, and (coupled from 2A) a -60M population with ~4.4->2.8 worker/retiree ratio and strained pension subsidies (~2.9T RMB, 10.1% of expenditure) that cap the transfer-financed consumption rebalancing. Japan 1995-2005 analogue averaged ~-0.1%. Against: 10 years is long; one pork/food spike at +5% adds 0.5pp to the decade average, an RMB devaluation imports food/energy inflation, and NBS has mild upward measurement/political bias against printing deflation. B = 72%.\nCORRELATION: mildly NEGATIVE in the standard channel (Chinese goods deflation exports into OECD core goods) but the tariff/fragmentation wall is precisely the mechanism that severs it, and both legs are driven by the same asymmetry (OECD builds physical capacity under labour scarcity; China has excess capacity and deficient demand). Net rho ≈ -0.12, so P(B|A) ≈ 0.72 x 0.90 ≈ 0.65. JOINT = 0.62 x 0.65 = 0.40, minus a ~1pp haircut for construction ambiguity that could be adjudicated against YES (a consumption-weighted rather than GDP-weighted OECD core, or a core-PCE-only reading, both of which lower the OECD leg). ANSWER 39.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:52.283Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat publishes the 2026 Structure of Earnings Survey microdata with median gross hourly earnings by NACE section, and the 2022-to-2026 change in NACE Q median hourly earnings is NEGATIVE or below the all-sector median change in at least 8 of the 12 largest EU members, despite published collective-agreement wage floors for care staff rising faster than the all-economy average in those members.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029 (SES 2026 reference year, publication typically ~30-36 months after)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CONFIRMS my central CQ1 claim (entrant dilution of the Q median) and would push CQ1 from 6 toward 3-4. The inverse observation — Q median hourly earnings outpacing the all-sector median by >=4pp over 2022-26 in most of those members — falsifies my composition argument and would roughly double CQ1 to 11-13, and would also raise CQ6-C by ~3pp since it implies the care wage channel reaches the core index.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"A published OECD or BIS decomposition of G7 core CPI/PCE attributes >=0.40pp/yr of 2027-2029 core inflation to electricity and grid-cost pass-through through non-energy channels (business-cost pass-through into services and processed food), i.e. the data-centre energy shock demonstrably crosses the core exclusion boundary at a scale above my +0.1-0.2pp estimate.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my 'core excludes the AI energy shock' driver, which is doing most of the work holding CQ6-C down. Would raise CQ6-C from 17 to ~26 (taken from B), raise the OECD leg of CQ2 from 62% to ~72% and CQ2 from 39 to ~45, and raise the C-consistent part of CQ4's distribution (P50 2.0 -> ~2.2).\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China's NBS publishes a rebased CPI basket (2026 base) under which 2027 and 2028 annual headline CPI both print >=1.8%, with the increase traceable to services and rent weights rather than to a food/pork cycle (i.e. core-equivalent services CPI >=2.0%), and household consumption rises above 42% of GDP in the same national accounts vintage.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my treatment of Chinese CPI as structurally floored near zero by the property adjustment, the low household income share and supply-side industrial policy. Drops the China conjunct of CQ2 from 72% to ~45% and CQ2 from 39 to ~26. Also raises my CQ3 China leg by ~0.4pp (P50 1.8 -> ~2.0) and shifts ~3pp from CQ6-D toward B.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An equity drawdown of >=20% (MSCI World, USD, closing basis) occurs in which the G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal sovereign yield FALLS by >=100bp between the equity peak date and the trough date — i.e. the classic flight-to-quality reasserts itself despite the 2A plumbing (convenience-yield erosion, basis-trade absorption, >100% debt/GDP).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the fiscal-dominance correlation structure behind CQ5. Drops P(yield condition | drawdown) from ~0.50 to ~0.30 and CQ5 from 27 to ~17; also lowers my CQ4 P90 from 3.1 to ~2.7 and shifts ~3pp from CQ6-C to CQ6-D (bond-market tolerance means repression is unnecessary and inflation is less likely to be accommodated).\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE announces a methodology change to, re-basing of, or discontinuation of the BofA MOVE Index, OR publishes a relative-volatility (log/percentage) variant that becomes the quoted benchmark; alternatively, Eurostat formally incorporates owner-occupied housing into the headline HICP with an announced start date inside the window.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Pure resolution-mechanics falsification of my lens's premise that the stated indices survive to resolve as written. A MOVE discontinuity makes CQ5 unresolvable-as-stated and I would move it to ~20 (annul risk resolves against YES on strict reading). HICP OOH inclusion adds +0.1-0.3pp to euro core, raising CQ2's OECD leg to ~68% (CQ2 to ~42) and CQ6-C by ~2pp — a probability shift caused entirely by index construction with no change in any underlying price.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 1 audit seat, effort 'inflation'."}}